  "body_html": "<h1>Grand Coalition Is Decisive under Unanimity</h1>\n<p><strong>Theorem.</strong> If a social welfare function $F$ (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.voting.swf\" href=\"voting/social_choice_voting_swf.html\">Social Welfare Function</a>)\nsatisfies unanimity (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.voting.unanimity\" href=\"voting/social_choice_voting_unanimity.html\">Unanimity (Pareto Axiom)</a>), then the grand\ncoalition $N \\subseteq N$ is decisive for $F$\n(<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.voting.is_decisive\" href=\"voting/social_choice_voting_is_decisive.html\">Decisive Coalition and Dictator</a>).</p>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Unanimity says: if every voter strictly prefers $a$ to $b$, society\ndoes too. Specialising the universal quantifier in $\\mathrm{IsDecisive}$ to\nthe universal coalition gives exactly that statement. $\\square$</p>\n<p>In Lean: <a class=\"lean-decl-ref\" href=\"https://github.com/gametheoryinlean/EconCSLib/blob/7c9a1688acedefe48ec89a442571f12d87a380db/EconCSLib/SocialChoice/Voting/Decisive.lean#L51\"><code>SocialChoice.Voting.unanimity_univ_isDecisive</code></a>.</p>\n<p>This is the <em>base case</em> of the decisive-coalition proof of Arrow's theorem\n(<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.voting.arrow_of_unanimity_iia\" href=\"voting/social_choice_voting_arrow_of_unanimity_iia.html\">Arrow&#x27;s Theorem via Decisive Coalitions</a>): start from the trivially\ndecisive grand coalition, then shrink it in cardinality using\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.voting.decisive_contraction\" href=\"voting/social_choice_voting_decisive_contraction.html\">Coalition Contraction — Splitting a Decisive Coalition</a> until you reach a singleton\ndictator.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MSZ Chapter 21] Maschler, Solan, and Zamir, <em>Game Theory</em>. Decisive-coalitions proof of Arrow.</li>\n</ul>",
